# transfermarkt-scraper — credentials and defaults
#
# Copy this file to `.env` next to the scripts and fill in what you use:
#
#     cp .env.example .env
#
# `.env` is in .gitignore. Keep it there. A secret passed on a command line
# is readable by anything that can run `ps` and lands in your shell history,
# which is why none of these has a flag you are expected to type.
#
# Precedence, highest first:
#
#     explicit CLI flag  >  exported environment variable  >  .env  >  default
#
# So a `.env` never overrides something you typed, and an already-exported
# variable (a CI secret, direnv, your shell profile) is never clobbered by a
# file you forgot to delete.
#
# Check what was picked up, without printing any secret:
#
#     python3 env_config.py
#
# Every variable below is one this code actually reads, and every variable
# the code reads is below — smoke_test.py asserts the two sets are equal in
# both directions, because a documented-but-unread variable is worse than an
# undocumented one.


# --- 2Captcha -------------------------------------------------------------
# One key, four separately-billed products: captcha solving, the Scraping
# Browser API, proxies and fingerprints. https://2captcha.com
#
# Worth knowing before you buy anything here — and note this advice REVERSED
# in v0.4.1, because the measurement behind the old version was taken from
# one network position and written up as a fact about the site.
#
# Transfermarkt sits behind AWS WAF on CloudFront. From a bare datacentre
# address, 8 of 10 consecutive requests on 2026-09-16 came back as an AWS
# WAF captcha rather than the page. From a 2Captcha proxy exit the same day,
# the real page came back with no solve needed at all. So what this key buys
# you here depends entirely on where you are running from:
#
#   * running from a residential or proxied exit — you may well need nothing,
#     which is what the old note described.
#   * running from a datacentre, a CI runner or a cloud VM — expect the
#     challenge, and then a proxy (or the Scraping Browser API, which carries
#     its own exit) is what gets you the page, and this key is what solves
#     the captcha if one still appears.
#
# Re-measure rather than trusting either number: `tools/waf_probe.sh` runs
# the ten requests above from wherever you are, and both figures are a
# property of an address on a day, not of the site.
TWOCAPTCHA_KEY=your_2captcha_api_key_here

# --- Scraping Browser API -------------------------------------------------
# A remote browser you do not run or patch, with a persistent profile and a
# chosen exit country. Used by --cdp-endpoint (playwright_scraper.py,
# puppeteer_scraper.py) and --cdp-url (scraper_api_client.py).
#
#   country-  picks the exit country. Not needed to REACH any of the five
#             verified hosts (see README "Locales") — none of them redirect
#             or gate by exit country, and TRANSFERMARKT_URL/--url is what
#             picks which locale you get, not this.
#   pid-      is a profile with persistent cookies. ONE live connection each,
#             capped per account — reuse pids rather than minting one per
#             run, and expect a 500 from the endpoint while another run
#             still holds this one.
#
# Note that Selenium cannot use this: chromedriver's `debuggerAddress` takes
# a bare host:port with nowhere to put a password. Playwright and pyppeteer
# authenticate on the WebSocket upgrade and work.
TRANSFERMARKT_CDP_ENDPOINT=ws://{login}-zone-scraping_browser-country-us-pid-{profileId}:{password}@cb.2captcha.com:9222

# --- Proxy ------------------------------------------------------------
# Not needed as measured (see the TWOCAPTCHA_KEY note above) — set only if a
# later run finds this has changed, or you want traffic spread across more
# than one address for --mode transfers --concurrency N.
#
# One URL. For a pool, use --proxy-file with one per line: a pool is what
# spreads a run's volume, and --concurrency without one just sends N times
# the traffic from a single address.
#
# The credentials here never reach the browser's command line — they go
# through the driver's own fields (Selenium cannot authenticate a proxy at
# all; see selenium_scraper.py). Logs keep the host and port and mask the
# rest, because which exit a run used is the point of the log and is not the
# secret.
TRANSFERMARKT_PROXY=http://{user}:{password}@us.proxy.2captcha.com:2334

# --- Default target ---------------------------------------------------
# Saves retyping --url. Any mode still needs its own --mode (and --club-id /
# --player-id, where that mode needs one); this only overrides the URL a
# mode would otherwise build from those.
#
# www.transfermarkt.com (English) by default. Five hosts are verified and
# accepted here -- .de, .world (Russian), .co.kr, .jp too, each normalised
# to English text on parse (see README "Locales") -- point this at one of
# those instead to default a run to that locale. Anything else Transfermarkt's
# own hreflang set lists (.es, .co.uk, ...) was not checked and is refused;
# see product_parser.HOSTS / LOCALE_BY_HOST and unsupported_reason().
TRANSFERMARKT_URL=https://www.transfermarkt.com/spieler-statistik/wertvollstespieler/marktwertetop

# Deliberately NOT here: an output prefix. `--out` already carries a
# non-empty default, so the loader — which only fills UNSET values — would
# never see it, and the variable would be silently inert: a setting that
# looks configurable and is not.
